          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>\"Doctor Zhivago\" and Controversy</strong><br>\nDoctor Zhivago, Pasternak's most famous novel, was written during the harsh years following World War II and published in 1957 in Italy after being rejected in the Soviet Union. The novel was critical of the Soviet regime and depicted the struggles of life during the Russian Revolution and Civil War. It led to international fame and controversy, culminating in the Soviet government pressuring him to decline the Nobel Prize he was awarded in 1958.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Prize and Government Backlash</strong><br>\nThe announcement that Pasternak was awarded the Nobel Prize for Literature in 1958 was met with hostility by the Soviet government. They forced Pasternak to refuse the prize, claiming his novel \"Doctor Zhivago\" was anti-Soviet propaganda. This refusal deeply saddened Pasternak, who valued the recognition but was compelled to prioritize his safety and that of his family.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Last Years and Legacy</strong><br>\nDespite the controversy, Pasternak continued to write until his death on May 30, 1960. He was not allowed to travel abroad and remained under surveillance. However, his legacy lived on through his works, which continued to be read worldwide. Pasternak's influence on Russian literature is undeniable, and \"Doctor Zhivago\" remains a defining work of 20th-century literature.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
