          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>\"Doctor Zhivago\" in the West</strong><br>\nDoctor Zhivago quickly became a success in the West, where it was seen as a powerful critique of Soviet ideology and was translated into numerous languages. The novel's portrayal of individual experiences during turbulent historical times resonated with a wide audience and cemented Pasternak's reputation as a major literary figure.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Relationships</strong><br>\nPasternak's personal life was as complex as his literary one. He was married twice and had several tumultuous affairs. His relationships often inspired his literary work, providing material for his deeply emotional and personal writing style. Despite the personal sacrifices he made for his art, including his relationships, Pasternak remained devoted to literature throughout his life.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence and Posthumous Recognition</strong><br>\nPasternak's writing has had a lasting impact on not only Russian literature but also on the global literary scene. After his death, \"Doctor Zhivago\" continued to gain recognition and was eventually published in the Soviet Union in 1988, during the period of glasnost. Today, Pasternak is remembered as a brave artist who remained true to his vision in the face of political oppression. His life and works continue to inspire new generations of readers and writers around the world.</p>\n"
              },
